<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nasir al-Din al-Tusi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1201–<a href="/years/1274">1274</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ṭūsī, Naṣīr al-Dīn al-Ṭūsī, Tusi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nasir al-Din al-Tusi was a Persian polymath, philosopher, scientist and Shi'ite scholar, born in Tus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. He worked under an Ismāʿīlī ruler at Alamūt, and when Hūlāgū Khān and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> captured Alamūt in 1256 he entered Hūlāgū's service as an astrologer and helped save numerous libraries from destruction. From 1259 an observatory was built to the west of Maragheh, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> Empire. He compiled the astronomical tables known as the Zīj al-Khānī, devised the Ṭūsī couple, criticised Ptolemaic astronomy and is often credited with making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">trigonometry</a> a discipline of its own. His works include The Nasirean Ethics of 1235.</p></div>
